{
  "summary": "This experiment established an image-only, single-profile baseline using a ResNet50 encoder with no aggregation or fusion, yielding a validation accuracy of ~20.8% (F1 ~17.2%), which is substantially lower than the RNA-only PCA baseline of ~48.5%. This initial observation suggests that, under this configuration, the image modality carries considerably weaker independent predictive signal than the RNA modality, though the comparison is confounded by differing encoder choices (learned ResNet50 vs. linear PCA) and cannot definitively isolate modality as the sole cause of the gap. It remains uncertain whether alternative image encoders, multiprofile aggregation, or a different architectural setup could substantially improve the image signal, and whether even a weak image contribution might still add value in a multimodal fusion setting."
}

